Peripheral Arterial Disease (Severe)
Geriatric & Frailty
Ischaemic rest pain relieved by dependency, Absent distal pulses, Punched-out painful ulcers/gangrene, ABI <0.40

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GEF-033
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, relentless pain in the foot preventing sleep, and the sudden appearance of a black discoloration on the great toe.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a frail patient presenting with severe, continuous foot pain at rest, explicitly relieved by hanging the leg off the bed, accompanied by a new, black tissue change. Urgent clinical assessment was mandated to evaluate for critical limb ischaemia and early gangrene, providing immediate analgesia and coordinating urgent vascular surgical review to prevent imminent limb loss prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My foot is screaming in pain, I have to sleep with it hanging off the side of the bed just to tolerate it, and now my toe is turning black."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a long-standing history of intermittent claudication that has rapidly progressed over the past two weeks to severe, continuous ischaemic rest pain, particularly in the toes of the left foot. They describe the pain as excruciating, explicitly noting it is exquisitely exacerbated by leg elevation and relieved only by adopting a dependent, hanging position (hanging the foot off the bed). They report their foot feels perishingly cold. Today, they noticed the formation of a deep, punched-out, painful, non-healing ischaemic ulcer on the lateral malleolus and the terrifying development of dry gangrene presenting as black, mummified tissue on the distal left great toe. They deny fever or rigors.
Functional Status: Highly restricted; cannot walk more than 10 meters due to severe calf and foot pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme risk of major amputation; living alone with severe pain causing profound sleep deprivation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 165/95 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Exhausted, in obvious severe distress, sitting on the edge of the bed with the left leg hanging down.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam of the lower extremities reveals diminished popliteal pulses and completely absent distal peripheral pulses (dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial) on the left. The left foot exhibits pale, perishingly cold, and shiny skin with a complete loss of hair over the toes and shins. Capillary refill time is severely delayed, exceeding 4 seconds. Buerger's test is strongly positive: the foot demonstrates striking pallor upon elevation and dependent rubor (a dusky red colour) when hanging. Inspection confirms a punched-out ulcer on the lateral malleolus and established dry gangrene (black eschar) on the tip of the left great toe. No signs of acute wet gangrene (fluctuance, foul odour, purulence). (A severely reduced Ankle-Brachial Index strictly less than 0.40 is an anticipated diagnostic tetrad finding).
Environmental Observations: A chair is positioned directly next to the bed where the patient sits to let the leg hang at night.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking regular aspirin and a statin, but the disease has progressed despite medical therapy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Peripheral Arterial Disease - Status: Acute Exacerbation (Critical Limb Ischaemia with early dry gangrene)
Differentials:

Acute Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The leg is pale, cold, and pulseless with tissue necrosis, rather than warm, swollen, red, and engorged, which is characteristic of venous obstruction.

Diabetic Peripheral Neuropathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The pain is exquisitely severe and position-dependent (ischaemic rest pain), rather than a constant painless numbness or burning "stocking" paresthesia; the pulses are also completely absent.

Acute Arterial Embolism (e.g., from AF) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The long, progressive history of claudication advancing to rest pain over weeks, rather than a sudden, explosive "6 Ps" presentation within minutes (though the end-stage appearance is similar).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (High risk of major limb amputation)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with textbook symptoms of critical limb ischaemia (CLI): severe ischaemic rest pain relieved by dependency, absent distal pulses, and the onset of tissue loss (dry gangrene). Telephone triage correctly identified a vascular emergency threatening limb viability. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would result in unmanageable, agonizing pain, extreme distress, and further irreversible tissue necrosis. This urgent after-hours face-to-face assessment confirmed the clinical diagnosis of CLI, ruled out wet gangrene (sepsis), provided immediate potent opioid analgesia to allow the patient to rest, and facilitated a direct, urgent referral to a vascular surgical team for potential revascularization or amputation planning.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered immediate strong oral analgesia (e.g., oxycodone 5mg) to manage the severe ischaemic rest pain tonight. Advised the patient to keep the leg in the dependent (hanging) position, as elevating it will immediately worsen the ischaemia and pain. Dressed the ulcer with a simple, dry, non-adherent dressing. Provided an urgent, marked referral letter for the Emergency Department vascular surgical team for the morning.
Positive Behavioral Change: (Not applicable in an acute limb-threatening scenario requiring urgent surgical review; focus is on immediate pain control and specialist referral).
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Protected the gangrenous toe with a loose, dry dressing to prevent mechanical trauma.
Follow-up: Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department in the morning for urgent Vascular Surgery review.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transport to the Emergency Department via ambulance tonight if the patient develops a high fever, if the black toe becomes hot, swollen, and foul-smelling (wet gangrene), or if the severe pain spreads further up the leg despite the strong painkillers.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The blood flow to your foot is critically low, resulting in the death of the tissue on your toe; this requires urgent specialist surgical evaluation in the hospital.
Clinical Course: The strong painkillers will help you sleep tonight, but the underlying blockage will not improve without surgical intervention (like a bypass or balloon procedure) to restore the blood flow.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Go to the Emergency Department immediately tonight if you develop a fever, if the toe starts oozing foul-smelling liquid, or if the pain becomes completely unmanageable. You must go to the hospital tomorrow morning to see the vascular surgeons.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The arteries in your leg have become so severely blocked that blood cannot reach your toes. This starvation of oxygen is causing the agonizing pain and turning the tissue black. Hanging your leg uses gravity to pull a tiny bit more blood down, which is why it relieves the pain slightly. We have given you strong painkillers tonight, but you need a vascular surgeon to try and unblock the pipes tomorrow.
Actionable Steps: Do not elevate your leg. Keep the foot dry and protected from bumping. Take the strong painkiller provided so you can rest, and go directly to the hospital tomorrow morning with the referral letter provided.

